<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><rss xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" version="2.0"><channel><title>World News, Today World News, Latest International News, World Breaking News, Trending News of World - Times of India</title><link>https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world</link><description>World News Today: Read the latest world news headlines, Current International breaking news world wide. In depth analysis and top news headlines world wide.</description><language>en-gb</language><lastBuildDate>2026-08-24T21:03:48+05:30</lastBuildDate><atom:link type="application/rss+xml" rel="self" href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/rssfeeds/296589292.cms"/><copyright>Copyright:(C) Mon, 24 Aug 2026 21:03:48 IST Bennett Coleman &amp; Co. Ltd, http://in.indiatimes.com/policyterms/1554651.cms</copyright><docs>http://syndication.indiatimes.com/</docs><image><title>World News, Today World News, Latest International News, World Breaking News, Trending News of World - Times of India</title><url>https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/507610.cms</url><link>https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world</link></image><item><title><![CDATA[Researchers develop 3D-printed replacements for missing pottery parts, embed sensors in them]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133459889.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Researchers at Koc University have developed a method that uses photogrammetry, 3D printing and embedded sensors to repair damaged ancient pottery. The customised 3D-printed sections can replace missing parts while monitoring temperature, humidity, pH and physical stress. Tested on maritime amphorae from Türkiye and Italy, the approach could help conservators track changes and support long-term preservation.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/researchers-developed-custom-3d-printed-replacements-for-missing-sections-of-ancient-pottery-and-embedded-sensors-inside-them-allowing-conservators-to-monitor-temperature-strain-and-environmental-damage/articleshow/133459889.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/researchers-developed-custom-3d-printed-replacements-for-missing-sections-of-ancient-pottery-and-embedded-sensors-inside-them-allowing-conservators-to-monitor-temperature-strain-and-environmental-damage/articleshow/133459889.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-24T20:50:13+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133463128,imgsize-106315.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[A black hole keeps tearing pieces off the same star, yet the star survives and comes back again]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133463351.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Astronomers have made fascinating observations of stars that are caught in a gravitational dance with supermassive black holes. As these stars approach, they lose material in a series of light bursts that fade over time. New research indicates that the stars' rapid initial spin is key to this phenomenon, as it limits excessive rotation during subsequent encounters with the black holes.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/some-stars-repeatedly-skim-past-supermassive-black-holes-lose-material-and-survive-scientists-now-think-their-unusually-rapid-spin-explains-why-each-returning-flare-becomes-progressively-dimmer/articleshow/133463351.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/some-stars-repeatedly-skim-past-supermassive-black-holes-lose-material-and-survive-scientists-now-think-their-unusually-rapid-spin-explains-why-each-returning-flare-becomes-progressively-dimmer/articleshow/133463351.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-24T20:50:03+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133463501,imgsize-323888.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Quote of the Day by Benjamin Franklin: "Well done is better than well said"]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133471191.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/quote-of-the-day-by-benjamin-franklin-well-done-is-better-than-well-said/articleshow/133471191.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/quote-of-the-day-by-benjamin-franklin-well-done-is-better-than-well-said/articleshow/133471191.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-24T20:19:08+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133471378,imgsize-524490.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Albany Police Department issues BOLO for a man who picked $30 from the floor while shopping at Walmart; faces backlash, says no charges will be filed]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133470460.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/albany-police-department-issues-bolo-for-a-man-who-picked-30-from-the-floor-while-shopping-at-walmart-faces-backlash-says-no-charges-will-be-filed/articleshow/133470460.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/albany-police-department-issues-bolo-for-a-man-who-picked-30-from-the-floor-while-shopping-at-walmart-faces-backlash-says-no-charges-will-be-filed/articleshow/133470460.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-24T19:47:39+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133470529,imgsize-124720.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Arthur creator Marc Brown sells Martha’s Vineyard home for $6.4 million after 37 years]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133461143.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Marc Brown, the creator of Arthur, along with his wife, has sold their beloved Martha's Vineyard estate for a substantial $6.4 million. This historic property, meticulously restored over thirty-seven years, was a sanctuary for Brown's creativity, where he penned half the Arthur books. With this sale, they conclude a significant chapter in their lives and have already acquired a new home in New York's Hudson Valley, ready for restoration.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1988-arthur-creator-marc-brown-bought-a-marthas-vineyard-estate-for-700000-after-37-years-and-writing-half-the-books-there-he-sold-it-for-6-4-million/articleshow/133461143.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1988-arthur-creator-marc-brown-bought-a-marthas-vineyard-estate-for-700000-after-37-years-and-writing-half-the-books-there-he-sold-it-for-6-4-million/articleshow/133461143.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-24T19:45:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133461309,imgsize-235848.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[In 1998, Costa Rica spread 12,000 tonnes of orange waste; 16 years later, forest biomass rose 176%]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133466780.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[In 1998, about 12,000 metric tonnes of orange peels and pulp were spread over a degraded pasture in Costa Rica as part of an unusual forest restoration experiment. Sixteen years later, researchers found that the site had developed into a dense forest, with richer soil, greater tree diversity and a 176% increase in aboveground biomass.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/in-1998-costa-rica-spread-12000-tonnes-of-orange-waste-over-degraded-pasture-16-years-later-tree-diversity-had-tripled-and-forest-biomass-was-176-higher/articleshow/133466780.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/in-1998-costa-rica-spread-12000-tonnes-of-orange-waste-over-degraded-pasture-16-years-later-tree-diversity-had-tripled-and-forest-biomass-was-176-higher/articleshow/133466780.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-24T19:20:28+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133468565,imgsize-312188.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[California family once refused to sell land needed for the 1960 Winter Olympics]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133468605.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/a-california-family-once-refused-to-sell-land-needed-for-the-1960-winter-olympics-the-state-offered-103000-but-the-owner-demanded-440000/articleshow/133468605.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/a-california-family-once-refused-to-sell-land-needed-for-the-1960-winter-olympics-the-state-offered-103000-but-the-owner-demanded-440000/articleshow/133468605.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-24T19:16:35+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure typ